## 28. Unknown Distance

Suppose:

> “The school is east of the hospital.”

The system knows:

```text
direction = East
```

but does not know:

```text
distance
```

It must not invent one.

Representation:

```text
direction = EAST
distance = UNKNOWN
```

This is a key principle:

> **Unknown information must remain unknown.**

## 29. Spatial Graphs

Spatial relationships naturally form graphs.

Example:

```text
Hospital
   │
   │ east
   ↓
School
   │
   │ north
   ↓
Park
```

The system can infer:

```text
Park is north-east of Hospital
```

even though this relationship was never explicitly stated.

## 30. Coordinate Propagation

Suppose:

```text
B is east of A.
C is north of B.
```

Set:

```text
A = (0,0)
```

Then:

```text
B = (1,0)
C = (1,1)
```

Therefore:

```text
C is north-east of A
```

If distances are known, the coordinates can preserve exact magnitudes.

## 31. Multiple Entities

Example:

```text
B is 5 km east of A.
C is 4 km north of B.
D is 3 km west of C.
```

Set:

```text
A = (0,0)
```

Then:

```text
B = (5,0)
C = (5,4)
D = (2,4)
```

Therefore:

```text
D is north-east of A
```

Distance:

```text
√(2² + 4²)
= √20
≈ 4.47 km
```

## 32. Spatial Constraints

Represent:

```text
B = A + East(5 km)
C = B + North(4 km)
```

Substitute:

```text
C = A + East(5 km) + North(4 km)
```

This becomes:

```text
C - A = (5,4)
```

The system can then calculate distance and direction.

## 33. Constraint Solving

Some problems contain unknowns.

Example:

```text
A = (0,0)
B = (x,4)
distance(A,B) = 5
```

Then:

```text
x² + 4² = 5²
x² + 16 = 25
x² = 9
x = ±3
```

Additional direction information can resolve the sign.

If B is east of A:

```text
x = +3
```

Therefore:

```text
B = (3,4)
```

## 34. Direction From Angle

For a displacement $(\Delta x, \Delta y)$, a continuous angle can be calculated using:

@math name="Bearing from Displacement" label="eq:atan2"
\theta = \operatorname{atan2}(\Delta y, \Delta x)
@/math

This is useful when the system needs precise orientation instead of only the eight coarse labels (N, NE, E, SE, ...). The system must define its angular convention explicitly.

## 35. Polar Coordinates

A point can also be represented as $(r, \theta)$, where $r$ is the distance and $\theta$ is the direction. For example, $r = 10$ km and $\theta = 45°$ represents a point 10 km away along that bearing.

Conversion to Cartesian coordinates, for the mathematical angle convention:

@math name="Polar to Cartesian (x)" label="eq:polar-to-cartesian-x"
x = r\cos\theta
@/math

@math name="Polar to Cartesian (y)" label="eq:polar-to-cartesian-y"
y = r\sin\theta
@/math

## 36. Cartesian vs Polar

### Cartesian

```text
(x, y)
```

Best for:

```text
vector addition
grid movement
coordinate constraints
```

### Polar

```text
(r, θ)
```

Best for:

```text
distance + direction
bearings
radial movement
```

A strong system should support both.
